<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	The Mac App Store build. Sandboxed, as the store requires.

	The entitlement set is the smallest one that lets the surviving modules
	work, and nothing is here speculatively. What is deliberately ABSENT is
	the more interesting half:

	  - No com.apple.security.files.downloads.read-write, and no temporary
	    exceptions for absolute paths. CoreTend's App Store build reaches
	    exactly the folders the user hands it, and asking for a blanket
	    Downloads grant would let it reach one they did not.
	  - No com.apple.security.network.client. The App Store build does not
	    check for updates — Apple delivers them — so it opens no sockets at
	    all. See AppCapabilities.canCheckForUpdates.
	  - No com.apple.security.automation.apple-events. Nothing here scripts
	    another application.

	Modules the sandbox removes (Storage cleanup, Applications, Privacy
	Cleaner, Integrity) are not present in this build at all, rather than
	present and failing. They are filtered out of the sidebar by
	AppCapabilities, and DistributionTests asserts that no route can reach
	one. So there is no entitlement here attempting to make them work.
	-->

	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Folders the user chooses in an open panel, and only those. Combined
	     with security-scoped bookmarks so a chosen folder survives a
	     relaunch without re-prompting. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>

	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
